    {
      "work_item_id": "m4-exact-projection-assertion-nondeterminism-successor",
      "stage_id": "M4",
      "status": "proposed",
      "state": "deliberately_non_gating",
      "why": "Owner ruling 2026-08-03. M4 is certified and its 98/98 bar stands; binding this would reopen a certified stage for a verifier defect rather than a substrate one. It is filed because a proof that can flake RED can also mask a real regression in the noise -- the danger is not the false alarm, it is the true alarm nobody believes."
    },
    {
      "work_item_id": "m5-p0-readiness-verifier",
      "stage_id": "M5",
      "status": "proposed",
      "state": "consumes_stage_exit_cannot_gate_it",
      "why": "record_role is private_verifier and the record is explicitly non-authoritative: it CONSUMES verified M3-M5 exits. Binding it as a child of the M5 exit would be circular — it cannot run until the exit it would gate has already been verified. It asserts no stage claim and gates nothing by construction."
    },
    {
      "work_item_id": "m5-qm-reference-shell-executable-rebind",
      "stage_id": "M5",
      "status": "proposed",
      "state": "deliberately_non_gating",
      "why": "Owner ruling 2026-08-02: apps/ioi-ai/ SCAFFOLDING gates M5, and the dormant adoption is the scaffolding. Binding the rebind would extend the M5 exit from 'the seed is adopted' to 'the seed runs against canonical owners' — a scope increase the ruling did not make. This is a decision, not a hedge: the scaffold is gating, its execution is not. The earlier transitive concern (that binding the rebind would pull in the seam) is moot now that the seam and the event successor are bound children in their own right."
    },
    {
      "work_item_id": "m5-thread-event-legacy-stream-migration-successor",
